<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="quiz-03">
  <title>Quiz 3</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The following work should be completed individually.
      Use of notes, calculators, or textbooks is not allowed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Show all work unless instructed otherwise.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each function <m>f(t)</m> below, find the Laplace transform <m>\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} = F(s)</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle{f(t) = 2t^3 + 5e^{-t} + 8}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow> F(s) \amp = 2 \cdot \frac{3!}{s^{3 + 1}} + 5 \cdot \frac{1}{s - (-1)} + 8 \cdot \frac{1}{s} </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \frac{12}{s^{4}} + \frac{5}{s + 1} + \frac{8}{s} </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle{f(t) = 2\sin(3t) + 3\cos(4t)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow> F(s) \amp = 2 \cdot \frac{3}{s^2 + 3^2} + 3 \cdot \frac{s}{s^2 + 4^2} </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \frac{6}{s^2 + 9} + \frac{3s}{s^2 + 16} </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <sidebyside widths="20% 40%">
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F(s)</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F(s)</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>u(t-a)f(t-a)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>e^{-as}F(s)</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>f'(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>sF(s) - f(0)</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>f''(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>s^2 F(s) - sf(0) - f'(0)</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </sidebyside>
  </page>


  <page>
    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow> u_1' = \frac{-y_2 g}{W(y_1, y_2)}, \qquad u_2' = \frac{y_1 g}{W(y_1, y_2)} </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use variation of parameters to find the general solution of <m>y'' - y = e^{x} + 4</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
            The associated homogenous equation is:
            <md>
                <mrow> y'' - y = 0, </mrow>
            </md>
            which has characteristic equation <m>f^2 - 1 = (r - 1)(r  + 1) = 0</m>, so <m>r = \pm 1</m>.
            Therefore, the general solution is:
            <md>
                <mrow> y_c = C_1 e^x + C_2 e^{-x}. </mrow>
            </md>
            Let <m>y_1 = e^x, y_2 = e^{-x}</m>.
            The Wronskian is:
            <md>
                <mrow> W \amp = \begin{vmatrix} e^x \amp e^{-x} \\ e^x \amp -e^{-x} \end{vmatrix} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = (e^x)(-e^{-x}) - (e^{-x})(e^x) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = -1 - 1 = -2. </mrow>
            </md>
            Now:
            <md>
                <mrow> u_1' \amp = \frac{-e^{-x}(e^x + 4)}{-2} = \frac{1 + 4e^{-x}}{2} \quad \Rightarrow \quad u_1 = \frac{x}{2} - 2e^{-x} </mrow>
                <mrow> u_2' \amp = \frac{e^x(e^x + 4)}{-2} = \frac{e^{2x} + 4e^{x}}{-2} \quad \Rightarrow \quad u_2 = \frac{-e^{2x}}{4} - 2e^x </mrow>
            </md>
            Now, the general solution to the nonhomogeneous equation is:
            <md>
                <mrow> y \amp = C_1e^x + C_2e^{-x} + \left(\frac{x}{2} - 2e^{-x}\right)e^x + \left(\frac{-e^{2x}}{4} - 2e^x\right)e^{-x} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = C_1e^x + C_2e^{-x} + \frac{xe^x}{2} - 2 - \frac{e^x}{4} - 2 </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = C_1e^x + C_2e^{-x} + \frac{xe^x}{2} - 4. </mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>